\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Bad primes versus vanishing primes}
The refuted conjecture conflates the \emph{bad primes} of the curve --- where the reduced
equation becomes singular, detected by $\Delta$ --- with the \emph{vanishing primes} of a point
--- where the reduction of $nP$ becomes the identity. Only the first is controlled by $\Delta$;
the second is controlled by the order of $\bar P$ in $E_N(\FF_\ell)$, governed by Frobenius,
not by the factorisation of $N$. This is the classical theory of elliptic divisibility
sequences; what the present analysis adds is exactness at the first two nontrivial layers.

\subsection{Why the counting laws are clean}
Both criteria reduce to root-counting for a cubic: $T^3+N$ at layer $2$, and $T^3+4N$ after
splitting off the free root $T = 0$ at layer $3$. Cube-root behaviour in $\FF_\ell$ is
dichotomous, and the totals $\sum_c \#V_2 = \ell$, $\sum_c \#V_3 = 2\ell-1$ are partition
identities, not analytic estimates. In Galois terms the layer-2 count at $\ell$ is the
splitting type of $\ell$ in the Kummer extension $\QQ(\sqrt[3]{N},\zeta_3)$.

\subsection{Consequences for factoring}
The naive attack fails for three independent reasons: denominators contain good primes, in fact
infinitely many; the factors $p$ and $q$ appear only sporadically and the larger factor
essentially never; and, structurally, the denominator profile below any bound $B$ is a function
of $N \bmod B!$, a residue class that also contains primes. The third reason is decisive: it is
a statement not about how the data happens to look but about how much data there is. Contrast
Lenstra's elliptic curve method, which succeeds precisely because it randomises over curves and
searches for smooth group orders --- information invisible in any fixed layer-$n$ criterion.

\subsection{Scope and hypotheses}
Every theorem requires $\ell \ge 5$ and $\ell \nmid N$. Both are necessary: the constants $4$,
$-9N$, $64$, $1728$ are supported on $\{2,3\}$, and at $\ell \mid N$ the cubic $T^3+N$
degenerates. The hypothesis $y \ne 0$ excludes $2$-torsion, $\psi_3(x) \ne 0$ excludes
$3$-torsion; over $\QQ$ these exclude only finitely many points on a given curve.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[The division-polynomial tower]
For every $n \ge 2$, every integral point $P = (x,y)$ of $E_N$ with $\psi_n(x,y) \ne 0$, and
every prime $\ell \ge 5$ with $\ell \nmid N$, $\ \ell \mid \den x(nP) \iff \ell \mid
\psi_n(x,y)$, where $\psi_n$ is the $n$-th division polynomial; moreover the exceptional
evaluation of the numerator $\varphi_n$ on the locus $\psi_n = 0$ is always of the form
$c_n N^k$ with $c_n$ composed of the primes $2$ and $3$ only.
\end{conjecture}

The evidence is the pair of layer-3 evaluations $\varphi_3 \equiv 64N^3$ on $x \equiv 0$ and
$\varphi_3 \equiv -1728N^3$ on $x^3 \equiv -4N$: the constants $2^6$ and $2^63^3$ are the
discriminant and $j$-invariant constants of the family, and they should recur at every layer
because $\varphi_n$ and $\psi_n^2$ are coprime up to the discriminant. Falsifiable form:
compute $\psi_4,\varphi_4$ and check whether the exceptional value of $\varphi_4$ on
$\psi_4 = 0$ involves a prime $\ge 5$.

\begin{conjecture}[Exact class counts and a Chebotarev law]
Let $r_n(N,\ell)$ be the number of residue classes $x \bmod \ell$ producing $\ell$ in
$\den x(nP)$. Then $\sum_{N \bmod \ell} r_n(N,\ell) = c_n\ell + O(1)$ with
$c_n = \deg\psi_n/(n-1)$, and for fixed $N$ the density of primes with $r_n(N,\ell) = k$ is the
Chebotarev density of the corresponding conjugacy class in $\Gal(\QQ(E_N[n])/\QQ)$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[The barrier at every layer]
For every $B$ and every semiprime $N = pq$ with $p,q > B$ there is a prime $M$ such that the
entire denominator profile $\{(n,\ell,x) : \ell \mid \den x(nP)\}$ restricted to $\ell \le B$
coincides for $E_N$ and $E_M$, at all layers simultaneously.
\end{conjecture}

Two further questions. How large must $B$ be before the denominator profile determines $N$
itself? Theorem~\ref{thm:barrier} shows $B < \min(p,q)$ is insufficient. And how much of this
survives outside the Mordell family: for $y^2 = x^3+Ax+B$ the analogous statements should hold
with $\{2,3\}$ replaced by the primes dividing the $j$-invariant denominators, and the barrier
should apply verbatim to any family whose criteria are polynomial congruences in the
parameters.

\section{Conclusion}

The ``only bad primes'' conjecture is false, and its failure is structural: a prime divides
$\den x(nP)$ precisely when the point reduces to a point of order dividing $n$, an event
governed by the division polynomials and independent of the discriminant. At the first two
nontrivial layers we have exact criteria ($x^3+N \equiv 0$ for doubling, $3x(x^3+4N) \equiv 0$
for tripling), exact residue-class counts ($1$ or $0$-or-$3$; $2$ or $1$-or-$4$), exact
averages ($1$ and $2-1/\ell$), exact activity densities, and a realisation theorem showing that
every prime $\ell \ge 5$ occurs with good reduction. The same locality that makes these
criteria clean makes them useless for factorisation: the whole profile below a bound $B$
depends only on $N \bmod B!$, and that residue class contains primes.

